PREFIX pair: <http://w3id.org/airiskkg/pair-ai#>
PREFIX beam: <http://w3id.org/beam/core#>

# Content categories flow with the data that carries them.
#
# The modeler annotates WHERE protected content enters (Rule R8: annotated base
# fact - only a human knows that a given store holds personal data). Whether it
# can REACH a later element is structural, so it is derived here by following
# beam:use -> beam:produce, rather than being hand-tagged on every element in
# between. The runner re-runs this to a fixed point, so one hop becomes the full
# transitive closure along the flow.
#
# Generic over the content-kind branch of the scheme (anything under
# pair:Information: SensitiveInformation, ConfidentialInformation,
# ExternalUserContent, GeneratedContent) rather than one query per category, so
# a new content category propagates without new machinery. The trust markers
# pair:UntrustedContent / pair:TrustedContent are deliberately NOT handled here:
# they are roots outside pair:Information, carry different semantics, and have
# their own rule with a guardrail barrier (propagation/untrusted_content.rq).
#
# Barrier: a redaction step. Content that passes through a step whose role is
# under pair:RedactionStep is treated as no longer carrying the category, which
# is what makes "add redaction here" a modelable mitigation rather than advice.
#
# Candidate framing (Rule R4): this derives REACHABILITY, not disclosure. That
# protected content can reach an output is a structural disposition; the finding
# built on it stays a candidate.

CONSTRUCT {
    ?downstream pair:containsDataCategory ?category .
}
WHERE {
    ?upstream pair:containsDataCategory ?category .
    ?category pair:subDataCategoryOf* pair:Information .

    ?step beam:use ?upstream ;
          beam:produce ?downstream .

    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
        ?step pair:playsRole ?stepRole .
        ?stepRole pair:subRoleOf* pair:RedactionStep .
    }
}
